WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.700
Secure sandboxing starts with the right expectation.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.920
It is containment, not magic.

3
00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:13.340
Continue to validate input, write defensive C++, update dependencies, and test parser behavior.

4
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:19.520
Use the sandbox to reduce what a risky component can access or affect when something still goes wrong.

5
00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:23.540
The boundary should be visible enough that engineers can explain it during design review.

6
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:29.100
Start with a threat model, then isolate risky work into lower-authority components.

7
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:39.520
Keep sensitive operations in a broker, grant only the filesystem, network, device, UI, and credential access required, and make every permission a documented decision.

8
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.880
Keep IPC narrow, validated, and capability-based.

9
00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:53.260
The broker should validate every request, issue scoped authority for approved actions, bound message size and rate, and reject unsupported behavior safely.

10
00:00:53.360 --> 00:01:00.400
Resource limits should cover CPU, memory, disk, handles, queues, and time.

11
00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.800
Finally, govern the lifecycle.

12
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:09.340
Design for crashes, cancellation, shutdown, and partial output.

13
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.560
Monitor denied operations and worker failures.

14
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:20.260
Review sandbox policy whenever features, dependencies, platforms, or trust boundaries change.

15
00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:25.400
Good sandboxing is an architecture discipline that stays alive after release.

16
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:33.580
It remains effective only when permissions, broker contracts, worker behavior, and monitoring are kept aligned with the real product.

17
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.400
When these habits become routine, sandboxing is easier to maintain across releases.
